In article <464@uucs1.UUCP>, rfp@uucs1.UUCP (rfp) writes:
> 3) Make the endgame harder by making the walls impenetrable to Xorns,
>    and the monsters immune to the ring of conflict.  Consider also
>    giving some of them stoning resistance (for those who don't normally
>    have it).
> 
> 6) Prevent troll abuse by not giving new objects to reincarnating trolls.
> 
> 7) Prevent scroll of genocide abuse by making monsters created with a
>    cursed scroll of genocide never leave a corpse.
> 
The problem with this sort of thing is that the loopholes are part of what
nethack is all about.  The great thing about nethack is its richness, and
all the weird and interesting things you can do.  If we get too excited
about closing all "loopholes" and "abuses", we end up turning nethack back
into a straight hack-and-slash game.  For example, I strongly dislike the
fact (rumor?) that the one sure way of keeping the wiz out of your way is
gone in pl10 (is there another one, anyone?).  If the only way to get out
is to keep beating the shit out of him, where's the strategy?

Polypiling is on the order of replicating wishes, but I'd like to be able
to get my gray dragon scale mail by fighting through a dozen gray dragons
if I really feel like it.  Making undead (especially wraiths and vampires)
nastier might help too.  Weren't wraiths real mothers back in D&D?  (I know,
this isn't D&D, but it's a bit odd that there are only about a dozen really
dangerous monsters once you're on your way up.)

> 4) Speaking of stoning, if the Wiz is carring a dead lizard, he should be
>    smart enough (and have time enough) to eat it - and blast that corpse
>    out of your hand with a spell.
> 
This is different -- ** IF ** the wiz has a dead lizard, he can save
himself from stoning.  But don't just cop out and always give him one,
or just make him stone resistant.

> 8) Give beginners a break on armor/weapon enchanting by giving a message
>    when a weapon or piece of armor is enchanted up to the point where
>    the next enchantment could lose it.  For example, when a +5 weapon
>    is enchanted to +6 (or +8 blessed scroll), the message might be:
>    "your athame almost disintegrates from the power of so much enchantment"
> 
Hmmm, gives away the identity of the scroll... It also resembles how
choking works today.  Want to start another source of endless debate?
My humble opinion on that sort of thing is that there will be other
weapons and other games, and a good hard slap teaches quicker than a
gentle warning...

> 9) An attempt to move into a pool really deserves a "go swimming?" check.
>    There should also be a water-walking/swimming intrinsic gained from
>    eating a water nymph, a crocodile, or an eel.  Of course if you can
>    and do go swimming, your scrolls go blank, etc.
> 
Cool, though it would be pretty easy to get (crocs are common).  If
you have to do in an eel or a kraken to get it, though, that could be
interesting.

> 10) If Orcrist is an artifact, surely Glamdring must be too!  Also, wasn't
>     Aragon's sword (the one that was broken and reforged) named Narsil?
>     (Tolkien types, please correct as needed.)
> 
Narsil or Anduril would do.

My contribution to the wish list, a fairly simple one, would be:

MAKE STRENGTH HEAL.  If hit points and energy points come back after
time, why shouldn't strength also?  I have gone for a LONG time (e.g.
all the way to Medusa, painfully) with a 12 strength because I *never*
found restore ability or anything else to bring strength back.

